{"name":"Broadway","hex":"#145775","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Broadway","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A street name, typically for a wide road: a specific broad way.","A place name for a settlement which grew up around such a road. For example, Broadway, Worcestershire, Broadway, Somerset."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The highest straight in poker, ace-king-queen-jack-ten."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Broadway"}],"entries":[{"title":"Broadway theatre","blocks":[{"text":"Broadway theatre is a theatre genre that consists of the theatrical performances presented in 41 professional theaters, each with 500 or more seats, in the Theater District and Lincoln Center along Broadway, in Midtown Manhattan, New York City. Broadway and London's West End together represent the highest commercial level of live theater in the English-speaking world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Various_Pictures_from_New_York_City_-_September%2C_2025.jpg/330px-Various_Pictures_from_New_York_City_-_September%2C_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadway_theatre","wikidata":"Q235065"},{"title":"Broadway (Manhattan)","blocks":[{"text":"Broadway is a street and major thoroughfare in the U.S. state of New York. The street runs from Battery Place at Bowling Green in the south of Manhattan for 13 mi (20.9 km) through the borough, over the Broadway Bridge, and 2 mi (3.2 km) through the Bronx, exiting north from New York City to run an additional 18 mi (29.0 km) through the Westchester County municipalities of Yonkers, Hastings-on-Hudson, Dobbs Ferry, Irvington, Tarrytown, and Sleepy Hollow, after which the road continues, but is no longer called \"Broadway\". The latter portion of Broadway north of the George Washington Bridge/I-95 underpass comprises a portion of U.S. Route 9."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Broadway_looking_north_from_48th_Street_Manhattan.jpeg/330px-Broadway_looking_north_from_48th_Street_Manhattan.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadway_(Manhattan)","wikidata":"Q11260"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Park_Theatre_interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Park_Theatre_interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The interior of Park Theatre, built in 1798","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APark_Theatre_interior.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Crookfinale.jpg/500px-Crookfinale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Crookfinale.jpg/1280px-Crookfinale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Crookfinale.jpg/500px-Crookfinale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Black Crook (1866), considered by some historians to be the first musical.[14] Poster for the 1873 revival by The Kiralfy Brothers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrookfinale.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Give_My_Regards_to_Broadway.jpg/500px-Give_My_Regards_to_Broadway.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Give_My_Regards_to_Broadway.jpg/500px-Give_My_Regards_to_Broadway.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sheet music to \"Give My Regards to Broadway\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGive_My_Regards_to_Broadway.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Victor_Herbert.jpg/500px-Victor_Herbert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Victor_Herbert.jpg/1280px-Victor_Herbert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Victor_Herbert.jpg/500px-Victor_Herbert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Victor Herbert","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVictor_Herbert.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Broadway_theatres_1920.jpg/500px-Broadway_theatres_1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Broadway_theatres_1920.jpg/500px-Broadway_theatres_1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Broadway north from 38th St., New York City, showing the Casino and Knickerbocker Theatres (\"Listen, Lester\", visible at lower right, played the Knickerbocker from December 23, 1918, to August 16, 1919), a sign pointing to Maxine Elliott's Theatre, which is out of view on 39th Street, and a sign advertising the Winter Garden Theatre, which is out of view at 50th Street. All but the Winter Garden are demolished. The old Metropolitan Opera House and the old Times Tower are visible on the left.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABroadway_theatres_1920.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/JudgeMagazine21Feb1925.jpg/500px-JudgeMagazine21Feb1925.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/JudgeMagazine21Feb1925.jpg/1280px-JudgeMagazine21Feb1925.jpg?utm_source=en.wik